 "notes": [
  "Same frozen 20-year dataset as a-exits-20y-v1, already seen once: this is exploration. A passing variant becomes a holdout candidate, not a production rule.",
  "D variants change one part of the current rule at a time; C variants measure what any stop is worth; P and N are grids around the A1-60 and A2 findings.",
  "R-multiples are not comparable across stop widths, so the champion is the passing variant with the highest mean net return per day held (capital efficiency).",
  "Benchmark: SPY bought at the open of the same entry day and sold at the same exit days and fractions; same costs on both, so excess = trade gross minus SPY gross.",
  "The excess t value uses monthly averages, because signals in the same month share one market; 3.0 is the Harvey, Liu and Zhu (2016) hurdle for many tested variants.",
  "Same pass bar as A-exits plus two market checks, frozen before the run."
 ]
